body:
A timber café with a sprawl of mismatched tables under the trees on the Dijksgracht, a short walk east of Centraal Station. It is open all year and has room inside, but summer is the point: on a warm afternoon the terrace fills up early, boats moor at the jetty and their crews order from the water, and the view runs across the Oosterdok to the green bulk of NEMO. Man-Man, Girls Who, Entree and AmsterdamNow all put it on their 2026 lists of sunny terraces.

The spot was never meant to be permanent. In 2020 Het Parool reported that Hannekes Boom stays on a lease renewed every five years, until the city settles a zoning plan for the Dijksgracht; the café is still there.

Look for:
- the jetty, where boats come alongside for a drink;
- the Oosterdok swing bridge right next door;
- string lights in the trees once it gets dark.

Few summer places start from less: a strip of quay along the Singelgracht, under a multi-storey car park on the Marnixstraat. Waterkant fills it with long tables right at the water's edge and a bar where you order your own drinks, then sit down with friends or with whoever has a free seat. The kitchen and the mood are Surinamese ("our Surinamese roots are the base", says its own site), and the music is always on.

Your Little Black Book calls it "a favourite that stays a favourite" and its Friday-afternoon drinks "legendary"; Man-Man says that when the temperature rises you sit here for hours, baking in the sun by the water. Evenings often roll on into a night out.

Look for:
- the parking garage floors stacked above the terrace;
- boats squeezing past on the Singelgracht;
- the Friday-afternoon drinks crowd.

Around 2005 the RAI wanted to do something with the land behind its halls, by the water, and hospitality entrepreneur Michel Pais turned it into a beach. Twenty seasons later Strandzuid draws thousands of people a year, from April to September, the RAI wrote on its anniversary in 2025. In winter the same spot becomes Zuidpool, a party venue.

The routine is simple, as Man-Man puts it: get a beer at the bar, drop onto a lounger in the sand and before you know it a few hours have gone. The water in front is a small pond on the edge of the Beatrixpark that connects to open water, so some guests arrive by boat (De Buik). It is not a bathing water: this beach is for lying on, not for swimming from.

Look for:
- loungers and sand right at the water's edge;
- the jetty bar;
- the RAI halls looming behind the beach.

At the very end of Zeeburgereiland, past the skatepark and the new towers, Kaap has run a free-spirited city beach since about 2020: a restaurant, an events stage, sand with swings, benches and play equipment, and views over the water. The kitchen is vegetarian and works with local produce, children get pancakes and lemonade, dogs are welcome, and on summer weekends people dance in the sand until late.

What it lacks is a swim. "They eat a salad, flop down on the sand and sigh: can you swim here too? No," owner Stefano Richetta told De Brug in 2026: it isn't allowed, and a fence keeps you from the water. That should change. From January 2027 the city turns the Oostpunt into a dune park, with a small beach and swimming water planned from summer 2027, and Kaap may stay at least until the end of 2028.

Look for:
- the swings in the sand;
- the wide water view out to the east;
- the fence that, for now, keeps everyone dry.

On an industrial quay in Nieuwendammerham, Hangar is an old hangar fitted out by designer Tim Immers, with one of the bigger terraces on the IJ: sand, tables and a view across the water to the old city and Centraal Station. Your Little Black Book describes a terrace where you can watch boats all afternoon, and if you come by boat you can moor. De Buik calls it the place for a cocktail on maybe the finest terrace on the IJ.

The rest of the Aambeeldstraat plays along. At no. 10 Hotel de Goudfazant, a restaurant in a former garage since 2006, started the street's reputation; Barracuda, a few doors on, had beach beds and outdoor showers on the quay in 2025 (De Buik). Some people take a dip off the quay. Don't: this is the IJ, a working shipping channel, and nobody tests the water.

Look for:
- the skyline of the old city across the water;
- the ferries and barges going by;
- the garage hall of Hotel de Goudfazant along the quay.

On summer weekends a steady stream of Amsterdammers cycles south along the Amstel, past the Riekermolen and the meadows, and stop in Ouderkerk aan de Amstel. The reward is a row of terraces on the Amstelzijde, the river's west bank, with boats mooring alongside. Café Restaurant Heisa has a big terrace right on the water and its own jetty; Brasserie Paardenburg has "maybe one of the most beautiful terraces on the Amstel"; Ron Gastrobar Indonesia and SAAM are next door, and across the little bridge Bakker Out has a small terrace by the water.

Your Little Black Book, written by a Ouderkerk native, puts it simply: some of the most beautiful terraces are here, on the Amstel, and they give you the feeling of being away from the city.

Look for:
- boats mooring in front of the terraces;
- the Riekermolen on the way;
- the Portuguese Jewish cemetery Beth Haim across the river.

Deep in the Vondelpark, the Groot Melkhuis has been a café since 1874, and, as its name says, it began as a place where milk was sold. Today it is a pavilion with a big terrace on the water and, right beside it, the playground that generations of Amsterdammers have swung in. "Which Amsterdammer hasn't sat on a swing there?" asks Vondelpark Info.

On a summer afternoon the terrace is all wooden picnic tables, red and white chairs and barrels turned into tables (Bart's Boekje), with buggies parked in rows and children running between the swings and their parents' lemonade. There is a buffet inside when you need lunch.

Look for:
- the playground right beside the terrace;
- the pond right in front of the terrace;
- the old pavilion itself, a café since 1874.

Where ships once slid into the IJ, Helling 7 now stands on the old slipway of the NDSM yard, among the steel of ships and cranes, in a building put together from materials reused from the wharf. The kitchen cooks over open fire. Daan Bonsen dreamt it up with a builder and an events company, writes Entree.

In summer it's the terrace you come for. Your Little Black Book calls it one of the most impressive in the city: spacious, high and with a panoramic view over the water and the Amsterdam skyline, and with sun all day. From your table you look out over the IJ and the long concrete Kraanspoor (Entree). Time Out put it on its 2026 list of the city's best restaurants with a view.

Look for:
- the Kraanspoor, the old crane track with an office building on top;
- ships and ferries on the IJ;
- reused wharf materials in the building.

In 2018 the neighbourhood enterprise De VerbroederIJ won a city competition for a car park on the Johan van Hasseltweg and got to use the plot for free, at first for four years, for a city beach, a vegetable and pick-your-own garden and a pavilion for neighbourhood events (NU.nl). It is still going strong in 2026.

Your Little Black Book describes a big terrace on the sunny side of the IJ that runs into a city beach with seats in the sand, and regular activities such as chi kung with your feet in the sand or a yoga class. De Buik calls it a slightly alternative place with a good heart and a lot of sand, great with children, affordable, and with half off the bill for Stadspas holders. The ferry from the Azartplein docks right next to it.

Look for:
- the ferry landing next to the sand;
- the vegetable garden;
- the agenda of free activities, from comedy to sport.

It takes a little finding: round the corner from the former Hermitage, now the H'ART Museum, you go through the gates of the Hoftuin and land, as bySam put it when it opened in spring 2017, in a haven of calm. Dignita Hoftuin has a big terrace in that inner garden, and on a summer morning it is one of the gentlest places in the centre to sit in the sun with coffee, a creative brunch or a slice of cake.

Your Little Black Book calls it one of Amsterdam's best-kept secrets, a place you only notice when you cycle past in the evening and see the garden lit up for a private party. The café is part of Stichting Dignita, which helps people in vulnerable positions build their own future: eating here gives someone else a chance, as the café puts it.

Look for:
- the gate from the Nieuwe Herengracht;
- the garden lit up on summer evenings;
- the canal right outside the gate.

Behind the Vondelpark, just past the Amstelveenseweg, Gent aan de Schinkel is the kind of neighbourhood café you can drop into all day. The bar is lined with Belgian beers, from light thirst-quenchers to heavier bottles for later in the evening, and the brasserie kitchen works with regional suppliers (AmsterdamNow, 2026).

In summer the draw is outside: the terrace stands directly on the Schinkel, the wide canal along the edge of Oud-Zuid, where you watch the boats go by. Regulars from the neighbourhood and walkers coming out of the park fill it on sunny evenings; Your Little Black Book calls it the ideal spot to spend an evening with friends, and indebuurt lists it among the places where you sit in the sun.

Look for:
- the Kabouterbrug just along the water, with a hotel room on a pole;
- boats on the Schinkel;
- the long Belgian beer list.

"Anno 1857" reads the white gable. Long before the Amsterdamse Bos, Meerzicht was a farm whose owners served tea to the walkers passing by. When the Bos was dug and planted from the 1930s, the other farms in the polder had to go; only Meerzicht was spared, for the sake of its tea room (Oneindig Noord-Holland). Three generations later it is a pancake house on the Koenenkade, near the Nieuwe Meer and the Bosbaan rowing course.

On a summer day the terrace fills with families. Your Little Black Book describes a roomy, well-kept playground with a zip line the children love, a deer meadow, and pancakes with bacon and syrup after a long walk; in summer, it adds, the place can swarm with children, and with wasps that like the sugar too.

Look for:
- the 1857 date on the gable;
- the deer meadow;
- an old John Deere tractor among the flowers.

In the new Amstelkwartier, a strip of grass runs along the Amstel where the river makes its wide bend at De Omval. On fine days, says the city, the lawn invites you to sit and lie down; rowing club RIC sits in the middle, with a few cafés around it. In July 2026 Het Parool's readers chose it as their first sunset spot: from the lawns you look out over the bend and, on a clear summer evening, watch the sun sink behind the city skyline. Your Little Black Book's advice: go early for a good spot.

It is so popular that the city treats it as one of Oost's summer hotspots, with temporary toilets, extra bins, daily cleaning and wardens on hot days. Some people climb into the river down the steps. It is not an official swimming spot: the water isn't tested and boats pass close by.

Look for:
- Rembrandt's Omval across the bend;
- the timber tower HAUT behind the lawn;
- rowers from RIC heading out on the evening water.

At the eastern end of the city, where tram 14 stops, the Flevopark is the big park many Amsterdammers have never been to. "Maybe not the best-known park, but really worth a visit," writes Your Little Black Book: you can picnic by the water and find shade under the trees. The water is the Nieuwe Diep, a broad stretch on the park's northern side, and the lawns run down to it.

On summer evenings groups of friends fire up barbecues on the big field by the old Jewish cemetery, where barbecuing is allowed (indebuurt, 2025). Along the water the old Flevopark pumping station now houses a small gin distillery, and at the park's edge the Flevoparkbad open-air pool is the place for a proper swim.

Look for:
- the wall of the Jewish cemetery Zeeburg;
- the black Angsthaas rabbit, left behind by a festival in 2018;
- the chalet-style pumping station by the water.

On the Java-eiland, next to the Verbindingsdam, a long lawn with a row of trees, a footpath and a cycle path lies between the houses and the water of the IJhaven. On hot days it turns into a beach without sand: towels, picnics and sun until late, with a view over the water towards Zeeburg. Oost-Online wrote in 2026 that it keeps getting more popular.

So popular that the city lists it as one of Oost's summer hotspots, with temporary toilets, extra bins, daily cleaning, wardens on hot days and a barbecue ban on part of the lawn. At the end of 2023 a strip of flowering grass and new trees went in, as a soft border between the neighbours and the crowds. People do jump in. Don't: the IJhaven is an active harbour for inland shipping, the city says swimming here is in principle not allowed, and the water isn't tested.

Look for:
- the Jan Schaeferbrug, a Parool readers' sunset spot;
- barges moored in the IJhaven;
- the little canals of the Java-eiland behind you.

On the Weesperzijde, near Café Hesp, a loading quay runs along the Amstel. On sunny days, says the city, it is a fine place to be by the water, and Oost knows it: on warm evenings the quay edge fills with people sitting with their legs over the river, drinks beside them, as boats and rowers pass.

The city counts it among Oost's summer hotspots and brings in temporary toilets, extra bins and wardens on hot days; there is a barbecue ban, and a line of buoys to stop pleasure boats mooring. The quay is meant for working boats, which is also why swimming is in principle not allowed here and the water isn't tested. Oost-Online notes that people do swim from the strip by Hesp's terrace and warns that the boats can be dangerous. We say: sit, don't swim.

Look for:
- Café Hesp's terrace on the corner;
- boats and rowers on the wide Amstel;
- the Berlagebrug a little to the south.

Take the free ferry from behind Centraal Station, step off in Noord, and next to the white wedge of the Eye film museum a big lawn runs down to the IJ. Your Little Black Book recommends it as a picnic spot: a large grassy field by the water where you sit and look out over the skyline of the old city.

The park is young. It was laid out on new land along the IJ, and in 2009 and 2010 the city planted it with an elm arboretum: about 70 trees of 32 kinds. On a summer evening the lawn fills with picnic blankets while ferries shuttle back and forth and river cruisers glide past. Look, don't swim: the IJ is a busy shipping channel with ferries crossing every few minutes.

Look for:
- the elm collection along the paths;
- the A'DAM Toren next door, with its swing at the top;
- the ferries crossing to Centraal.
